The baseline is a single plaintext add, so there is little to remove. The cost is dominated by encoding the plaintext vector (~2.5 ms) plus the add. Any change that keeps the same operation counts is normalized away, so I'm submitting the minimal kernel unchanged in structure. I don't expect it to beat the baseline, but nothing cheaper is expressible: there are no multiplications, rotations or rescales to remove.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 + p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline is already one plaintext encode plus one add, so nothing can be removed. This attempt rewrites the add as `-((-x0) - p)`. It is a structurally different circuit, but I expect it to be no faster, because the two negations are extra work. I don't see a cheaper circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext n = -x0;
  Ciphertext m = n - p;
  Ciphertext y = -m;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The negation variant was slower (5.29 ms against 4.64 ms) because it added two extra operations. The baseline's single plaintext add is already the minimum for this function. Every cheaper circuit would need fewer operations than one add, and that would drop the plaintext term, so I am resubmitting the minimal kernel.

